FORMULATION, FLAVORING & INGREDIENTS
Beverage Formulation, Flavoring and Ingredients
A perfectly created beverage formula starts here.
The recipe for Success
Why have your beverage formulated by Big Brands?
The main reason to have your beverage formulated by the experts at Big Brands is that Big Brands is BOTH a beverage formulator and copacker. That means, we understand how to apply your beverage formula in a manufacturing setting. Not only is the formulator knowledgeable about the science behind your formula, but the production manager also makes recommendations based on the packing requirements of your beverage product and are familiar with lab batching, filling and packaging. We keep things all in the same place and that's why you should choose to have Big Brands co-pack and formulate your beverage project.
If you are starting out in the beverage development process, chances are you are using a recipe to craft your perfect home brewed beverage. To have your beverage product mass produced for commercialization, your home recipe or beverage idea is taken to a Food Scientist who provides an industrial recipe or beverage formula for making your product in mass quantities. The beverage formula has very specific instructions on how to batch, blend and copack your beverage product. The beverage formulation process will include packing requirements, shelf life or best by standards, heating and cooling requirements, ingredients, flavors, mixing instructions, pH, brix, and finished product storage details. A manufacturing ready beverage formula has the instructions for the beverage copacker. Your beverage formulas from Big Brands also includes a nutritional panel or supplement facts panel.
Need a manufacturing ready beverage formula?
If you are needing a manufacturing ready beverage formula, Big Brands is ready to help you formulate your beverage project. We provide beverage formulation for a variety of beverage projects from nonalcoholic beverages, to alcoholic beverages, to functional powders, and CBD drinks.
Need a pilot run or test run of your beverage?
If you are looking to do a trial run of your new product, we have this service available. A pilot run is subject to availability, unless it conducted as a preparation step for a full production run. When discussing your beverage project needs, make sure to mention if you need a pilot run and requirements.
Beverage Ingredients
Sourcing ingredients for your beverage formula are similar to finding ingredients for your recipe, some you already have, while other ingredients you have to go shopping for at the regular store or specialty store. Big Brands will source your ingredients for your beverage product for you. By having our beverage experts available for your project, you can focus on building and growing your brand.
Some common ingredients include:
* Functional - vitamins, electrolytes, and minerals
* Caffeine
* Sweeteners - sugar, sucrose, glucose, and fructose, inositol, Stevia, Chicory root, sucralos, and monk fruit
* Stabilizers
* Blends - vitamin blends, functional drink blends, and proprietary blends
* Brewing materials - hops, yeast, malts, yeast and spices
* Juices - blends, purees and concentrates
* Botanicals and Adaptogens
* Colors
* Specialty classifications - organic , Kosher
* Coffees and teas
* Preservatives
Ingredients can be available in liquid or powder form.
Beverage Flavors
To add to the flavor profile and aroma of a non-alcoholic product, an alcohol flavored additive can be added to your beverage. By adding an alcohol flavor, the alcohol version can be mimicked, but the finished product becomes non-alcoholic. For example, an alcohol free spirits are mostly flavor based used to imitate the original product. These unique products are sometimes refereed to as artisanal and alcohol flavored non-alcoholic beverages.
What is an Alcoholic Beverage?
Big Brands copacks fermented, brewed, and distilled alcoholic beverages. Types of alcoholic beverages include fermented alcoholic beverages produced by natural fermentation, such as wine and beer. With wine, grapes are fermented and with beer, barley, wheat or other grains are fermented. Fermented drinks are also called undistilled drinks. The process of fermentation of a beverage includes bacteria or yeast chemically converting sugar into ethanol.
